  "response": "## The most dangerous thing: `sendPayout` can pay someone twice, and nobody would know\n\n**`src/legacy/payouts.ts:23-42`**. Scan entries crime_00007 (Double Jeopardy, unsafe retry) and crime_00011 (Catch and Release, swallowed error). The two findings are one defect at the same call site, and I'm ranking them together.\n\n### Evidence\n\n```ts\n// payouts.ts:13\n// TODO: this needs an idempotency key before the next audit\n\nexport async function sendPayout(payout: Payout): Promise<boolean> {\n  let attempt = 0;\n  while (attempt < 5) {\n    attempt += 1;\n    try {\n      const res = await fetch(\"https://payouts.internal/v1/send\", {\n        method: \"POST\",\n        body: JSON.stringify({\n          destination: payout.destination,\n          amountCents: payout.amountCents,\n          at: Date.now(),\n        }),\n      });\n      if (res.ok) return true;\n    } catch {\n      // swallowed: the caller cannot tell a failure from a refusal\n    }\n  }\n  return false;\n}\n```\n\nFrom the scan: *\"retried mutation — fetch at line 28 (HTTP POST)\"*, *\"missing safeguard (primary): no idempotency or deduplication key\"*, *\"also absent: error classification, delay or backoff, jitter, timeout or cancellation\"*, and *\"the catch declares no binding, so the error object is not even available to the handler.\"*\n\nReading the code shows something the scan doesn't mention, and it makes the problem worse:\n- **`payout.id` is never sent.** The provider gets only a destination and an amount, so it has nothing it could use to match a retry to the first attempt.\n- **`at: Date.now()` changes on every attempt.** Each retry's request body differs from the last one, so even a provider that deduplicates on identical bodies would treat each retry as a new payout.\n- **Every non-2xx response is retried immediately with no delay**, including permanent rejections and ambiguous 5xx responses that may come back after the payout already went through.\n\n### What a customer would experience\n\nSay the provider commits the transfer, but the connection drops or times out before the response arrives. `fetch` throws, the empty `catch` hides it, and the loop sends the payout again, up to 5 times in total. The recipient gets paid two to five times. The business only finds out when reconciliation fails or the recipient reports it, and money that has already gone out is often hard to get back.\n\nThe reverse also happens. If every attempt fails, the function returns `false` with no error and no log, so the cause is lost. The recipient isn't paid, and nobody can tell whether the provider refused the payout, was down, or quietly accepted it.\n\n### Why it ranks above what the scan puts near the top\n\nThe scan's top three are the `Basket` contract drift (crime_00001), the \"enterprise\" literal scatter (crime_00002), and a test with no assertions (crime_00003). All three are near the top because they score `recency: 1` (or 0.71). That reflects how recently the files were changed, not how much damage they could do. `sendPayout` scores `recency: 0`, so the recency weighting pushed it down to seventh place.\n\n- **`Basket` drift (crime_00001):** Two type declarations disagree, which causes compile errors and possibly wrong totals in code that's actively being worked on. It's real, but it's caught early: the code is under development and covered by tests (`test_gap: 0`), and it moves no money by itself.\n- **The literal scatter and the weak tests:** These are maintenance problems. They make future mistakes more likely, but they don't move money incorrectly today.\n- **`settleBatch` (crime_00008):** This is the closest rival and has the same retry-plus-swallowed-error shape. It still ranks below `sendPayout` for two reasons. It sends `ledgerId`, so the provider at least has something it could deduplicate on. And it settles against a payment provider, where duplicates usually get caught during reconciliation, whereas a payout sends money out to a third party.\n- **The other high-severity findings:** The mixed UTC/local dates (crime_00004) and the duplicated admin rule (crime_00005) can cause wrong dates or authorization drift, but neither one is an irreversible money movement.\n\n`sendPayout` is the only finding where one transient network error sends real money to an outside party more than once, with nothing logged. It also has `test_gap: 0.75`, and its own comments admit the problem: `TODO: this needs an idempotency key before the next audit` and `nobody has owned this file since the migration`.\n\n### Fix\n\n1. Send a stable idempotency key derived from `payout.id` with every attempt, for example in an `Idempotency-Key` header, and include `id` in the request body.\n2. Generate `at` once, before the loop, so every attempt sends the same body.\n3. Retry only on network errors and specific retryable status codes, with backoff between attempts.\n4. Bind the caught error and either log it or rethrow it.\n5. Add a test that simulates a commit followed by a timeout and checks that the provider is called with the same key every time.",
